---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
---

<Layout
  title="The LIT Bible | Liberating Scripture Collective"
  description="The Liberation and Inclusion Translation (LIT Bible) is a trauma-informed, liberationist New Testament translation from the Greek, freely available at litbible.net. Created by Brandon Vélez Johnson."
  canonical="https://liberatingscripture.org/lit-bible/"
>
  <Fragment slot="head">
    <script
      type="application/ld+json"
      set:html={JSON.stringify({
        "@context": "https://schema.org",
        "@graph": [
          {
            "@type": "WebPage",
            "@id": "https://liberatingscripture.org/lit-bible/#webpage",
            url: "https://liberatingscripture.org/lit-bible/",
            name: "The LIT Bible | Liberating Scripture Collective",
            isPartOf: { "@id": "https://liberatingscripture.org/#website" },
            about: { "@id": "https://litbible.net/#translation" },
          },
          {
            "@type": "CreativeWork",
            "@id": "https://litbible.net/#translation",
            name: "Liberation and Inclusion Translation",
            alternateName: "LIT Bible",
            author: {
              "@type": "Person",
              name: "Brandon C. Vélez Johnson",
              url: "https://litbible.net/about",
            },
            publisher: {
              "@type": "Organization",
              name: "Liberating Scripture Collective",
              url: "https://liberatingscripture.org",
            },
            url: "https://litbible.net",
            inLanguage: "en",
            license: "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/",
            genre: "Bible translation",
            description:
              "A trauma-informed, liberationist New Testament translation from the Greek, freely available online with extensive footnotes and annotations.",
          },
        ],
      })}
    />
  </Fragment>

  <div class="lit-page">
    <header class="lit-hero">
      <div class="lit-hero__inner content">
        <p class="eyebrow">Flagship Translation</p>
        <h1>The Liberation and Inclusion Translation</h1>
        <p class="lede lit-hero__lede">
          A trauma-informed, liberationist Bible translation from the
          original language: freely available, thoroughly annotated, and built to surface
          what traditional translations obscure.
        </p>
        <div class="lit-hero__actions">
          <a
            href="https://litbible.net/read"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noopener noreferrer"
            class="btn btn--green"
          >
            <span>Read the LIT Bible ↗</span>
          </a>
          <a
            href="https://litbible.net"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noopener noreferrer"
            class="btn btn--hero-outline"
          >
            <span>Visit litbible.net ↗</span>
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </header>

    <div class="lit-body content">
      <section class="lit-section" aria-labelledby="lit-what">
        <h2 id="lit-what">What the LIT Bible is</h2>
        <p>
          The LIT Bible is a New Testament translation by
          <a href="https://litbible.net/about" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"
            >Brandon Vélez Johnson</a
          >, translated word-by-word from the Greek with attention to grammar,
          syntax, and historical context. It is not a paraphrase and not what most "literal" translations are: oversimplified attempts to substitute predetermined English words for Greek ones. It's an annotated translation where the interpretive work
          is made visible rather than hidden behind a facade of neutrality.
        </p>
        <p>
          The full name—Liberation and Inclusion Translation—reflects the
          conviction that the Bible's core movements are liberating, restorative,
          and radically inclusive, and that how we translate shapes how people
          experience God, themselves, and one another.
        </p>
        <p>
          The LIT Bible is published under a
          <a
            href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noopener noreferrer"
            >Creative Commons BY-NC-ND 4.0</a
          > license, ensuring it remains freely accessible for non-commercial use.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="lit-section" aria-labelledby="lit-approach">
        <h2 id="lit-approach">Translation approach</h2>
        <p>
          Every translation is an interpretation, and interpretation always
          reflects values. Rather than disguising those values, the LIT names
          them so readers can engage the text with awareness.
        </p>

        <h3>Faithfulness to the text</h3>
        <p>
          Translation choices are grounded in grammar, syntax, and historical
          context, not doctrinal shortcuts. Where a choice might raise
          questions, footnotes show the Greek term, its range of meanings, and
          the reasoning behind the rendering.
        </p>

        <h3>Clarity over religious jargon</h3>
        <p>
          Traditional theological terms are reconsidered when they obscure
          meaning, import later doctrine, or soften the text's original force.
          You'll see terms rendered in ways that may feel unfamiliar at first,
          because the goal is to let the original meaning breathe.
        </p>

        <h3>Attention to harm</h3>
        <p>
          Where scripture has been used to shame, exclude, or justify harm, the
          translation asks whether that harm comes from the text itself or from
          how it has been rendered and taught.
        </p>

        <h3>God looks like Jesus</h3>
        <p>
          The character of God is interpreted consistently with Jesus' life,
          teaching, and self-giving love rather than through abstract or
          punitive frameworks.
        </p>

        <h3>Belonging</h3>
        <p>
          The translation takes seriously the Bible's recurring insistence that belonging is always bigger than we've been taught and that the truest thing about humanity is our belovedness.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="lit-section" aria-labelledby="lit-examples">
        <h2 id="lit-examples">Why the language sounds different</h2>
        <p>
          Many English translations rely on traditional church vocabulary that
          has accumulated centuries of doctrinal meaning. The LIT often lets go
          of familiar phrasing to communicate what the Greek is actually doing
          in context.
        </p>
        <div class="lit-examples">
          <div class="lit-example">
            <p class="lit-example__greek"><em>ekklēsia</em></p>
            <p class="lit-example__traditional">Traditional: "church"</p>
            <p class="lit-example__lit">LIT: <strong>"called community"</strong></p>
          </div>
          <div class="lit-example">
            <p class="lit-example__greek"><em>pneuma</em></p>
            <p class="lit-example__traditional">Traditional: "spirit"</p>
            <p class="lit-example__lit">LIT: <strong>"life-breath"</strong></p>
          </div>
          <div class="lit-example">
            <p class="lit-example__greek"><em>euangelion</em></p>
            <p class="lit-example__traditional">Traditional: "gospel"</p>
            <p class="lit-example__lit">LIT: <strong>"triumphant message"</strong></p>
          </div>
          <div class="lit-example">
            <p class="lit-example__greek"><em>sōtērion</em></p>
            <p class="lit-example__traditional">Traditional: "salvation"</p>
            <p class="lit-example__lit">LIT: <strong>"liberation"</strong></p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <p>
          These choices aren't meant to be provocative. They're attempts to
          surface what the text was doing socially, relationally, and
          politically before religious shorthand smoothed it over.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="lit-section" aria-labelledby="lit-available">
        <h2 id="lit-available">What's available</h2>
        <p>
          The translation lives at
          <a
            href="https://litbible.net"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noopener noreferrer">litbible.net</a
          >, where it is freely available to read, search, and study.
          The site includes:
        </p>
        <div class="lit-features">
          <div class="lit-feature">
            <h3>The translation itself</h3>
            <p>
              24 of 27 New Testament books are currently published, with all 260
              chapters planned for completion by 2027. Every chapter includes
              extensive footnotes explaining translation decisions.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="lit-feature">
            <h3>Full-text search</h3>
            <p>
              Search across every published chapter by keyword, topic, or Greek
              term.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="lit-feature">
            <h3>Multiple reading modes</h3>
            <p>
              Read with or without footnotes, adjust text size and theme, and
              navigate by book, chapter, or search results.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="lit-feature">
            <h3>Translation commitments</h3>
            <p>
              A transparent account of the values and principles guiding every
              translation decision.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="lit-feature">
            <h3>Glossary</h3>
            <p>
              Key Greek terms and their LIT renderings, with explanations of
              why the translation departs from traditional vocabulary.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="lit-feature">
            <h3>Articles</h3>
            <p>
              Longer explorations of specific translation decisions, themes, and
              the scholarship behind them.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="lit-section" aria-labelledby="lit-relationship">
        <h2 id="lit-relationship">The LIT Bible and the Collective</h2>
        <p>
          The LIT Bible is the flagship project of the Liberating Scripture
          Collective. The translation came first; it's the seed from which the
          rest of the Collective's work grew. LSC was founded to support and
          steward the LIT Bible and build the wider ecosystem around it:
          teaching, public conversation, spiritual accompaniment, and community.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>litbible.net</strong> is where the translation lives.
          <strong>liberatingscripture.org</strong> is where the organization
          lives. The two sites are companions, different
          homes for different parts of the same work.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="lit-section" aria-labelledby="lit-cta">
        <h2 id="lit-cta">Explore the translation</h2>
        <p>
          The best way to understand the LIT Bible is to read it. Start with a
          familiar passage and notice what feels different and why.
        </p>
        <div class="lit-actions">
          <a
            href="https://litbible.net/read"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noopener noreferrer"
            class="btn"
          >
            <span>Read the LIT Bible ↗</span>
          </a>
          <a
            href="https://litbible.net/about"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noopener noreferrer"
            class="btn btn--outline"
          >
            <span>About the translator ↗</span>
          </a>
          <a
            href="https://litbible.net/translation-commitments"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noopener noreferrer"
            class="btn btn--outline"
          >
            <span>Translation commitments ↗</span>
          </a>
          <a href="/support/" class="btn btn--outline">
            <span>Support the work</span>
          </a>
        </div>
      </section>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>

<style>
  /* ---- Hero ---- */
  .lit-hero {
    background: var(--green);
    padding: clamp(3.5rem, 7vw, 6rem) 0;
  }

  .lit-hero__inner {
    max-width: 64ch;
  }

  .lit-hero h1 {
    font-family: "Crimson Text", Georgia, serif;
    font-size: clamp(2.2rem, 4.5vw, 3.8rem);
    color: var(--cream);
    margin: 0.75rem 0 1.25rem;
  }

  .lit-hero .eyebrow {
    color: var(--cream);
  }

  .lit-hero .lede {
    color: var(--cream);
  }

  .lit-hero__actions {
    display: flex;
    gap: 1rem;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    margin-top: 2rem;
  }

  .btn--hero-outline {
    background: transparent;
    border-color: var(--cream);
    color: var(--cream);
  }

  .btn--hero-outline::before {
    background: var(--cream);
  }

  .btn--hero-outline:hover,
  .btn--hero-outline:focus-visible {
    color: var(--green);
  }

  /* ---- Body ---- */
  .lit-body {
    padding: clamp(3rem, 6vw, 5rem) 0;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 3.5rem;
  }

  .lit-section {
    max-width: 64ch;
    margin-inline: auto;
  }

  .lit-section h2 {
    font-size: clamp(1.5rem, 2.5vw, 2rem);
    margin: 0 0 1rem;
    padding-bottom: 0.5rem;
    border-bottom: 2px solid var(--green);
    display: inline-block;
  }

  .lit-section h3 {
    font-size: clamp(1.15rem, 1.8vw, 1.35rem);
    font-weight: 600;
    margin: 2rem 0 0.4rem;
  }

  .lit-section h3:first-of-type {
    margin-top: 1.5rem;
  }

  .lit-section p {
    max-width: 68ch;
  }

  /* ---- Translation examples grid ---- */
  .lit-examples {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fill, minmax(220px, 1fr));
    gap: 1rem;
    margin: 1.5rem 0;
  }

  .lit-example {
    background: var(--surface-raised);
    border-radius: 12px;
    padding: 1.25rem 1.5rem;
    box-shadow: 0 2px 12px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.06);
  }

  .lit-example p {
    margin: 0 0 0.35rem 0;
    font-size: 0.95rem;
    line-height: 1.4;
  }

  .lit-example p:last-child {
    margin-bottom: 0;
  }

  .lit-example__greek {
    font-family: "Crimson Text", Georgia, serif;
    font-size: 1.15rem !important;
    color: var(--green);
    margin-bottom: 0.5rem !important;
  }

  .lit-example__traditional {
    color: rgba(29, 35, 28, 0.55);
    font-size: 0.85rem !important;
  }

  .lit-example__lit {
    font-size: 1rem !important;
    color: var(--text-strong);
  }

  /* ---- Features grid ---- */
  .lit-features {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fill, minmax(260px, 1fr));
    gap: 1.25rem;
    margin: 1.5rem 0;
  }

  .lit-feature {
    background: var(--surface-raised);
    border-radius: 12px;
    padding: 1.5rem;
    box-shadow: 0 2px 12px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.06);
  }

  .lit-feature h3 {
    font-size: 1.1rem !important;
    margin: 0 0 0.5rem !important;
    color: var(--green);
  }

  .lit-feature p {
    font-size: 0.95rem;
    margin: 0;
    line-height: 1.5;
  }

  /* ---- CTA section ---- */
  .lit-actions {
    display: flex;
    gap: 1rem;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    margin-top: 1.5rem;
  }

  .lit-actions .btn {
    padding: 10px 20px;
  }

  /* ---- Dark mode adjustments ---- */
  @media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) {
    :root:not([data-theme="light"]) .lit-example__traditional {
      color: rgba(228, 226, 220, 0.45);
    }
  }

  :root[data-theme="dark"] .lit-example__traditional {
    color: rgba(228, 226, 220, 0.45);
  }
</style>
